Z||000036||88formula||06-28-2001||06:18 PM||||Shaun41178 The horsepower goes up from 177 to 200 with the combination I have now when I add headers instead of the parted manifolds. That’s a 13 horsepower difference not 20. I don’t believe the FOCOA headers can add 20 horsepower either unless they were perfectly tuned headers with an open exhaust.   <P>The program is more useful for getting an approximate idea of what kind of power to expect from certain engine combinations. It’s nothing that should be engraved in stone. It's also great for testing and comparing different camshafts, finding the compression ratio, and many other factors. In fact I think it’s the best tool to compare camshafts with without spending thousands on a dynamometer and engine parts.  It's just a tool that I use and it's a whole lot better than guessing or using a chart or written decryption from the manufacturer. I've also found it to be very accurate when comparing to real life figures when I have the right information. In Steven’s case I am missing one critical figure and that is his head flow data. I have the stock head flow data so any engine that has stock heads I can get more accurate results with.<P>I look at the program for what it can do not what it cannot do. The real thing is just out of my league. <BR>||||reg||1
